5307e-07,-0.0008155357209034264,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-7.152555099310121e-07,-3.576278118089249e-07,-5.364403477869928e-06,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,0.0,-1.9550132492440753e-05,-3.2186455882765586e-06,0.0,-0.00010990492592100054,-0.7097537517547607,0.0,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-0.00034528967808000743,-1.1920922133867862e-06,-2.4324984550476074,-1.2191543579101562,-0.6418811678886414,-0.03986351191997528,-0.2567930221557617,-5.960462772236497e-07,0.0,-0.1775730699300766,0.0,-0.00021062063751742244,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.3616313934326172,-3.6954811548639555e-06,-9.059865078597795e-06,-5.483612312673358e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,2461,1246,1657,2750,315,400,87,3,304,400,58,15,26266,2493,94280,374,57960,15940,47822,481,220,16,92,11520,15940,220,21,87,8,284,1124,9407,47822,481,220,16,92,11520,9407,856,15087,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFor how many values of $x$ in $[0,\\pi]$ is $\\sin^{ - 1}(\\sin 6x) = \\cos^{ - 1}(\\cos x)$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d451e8ed852c8267f6656af3841461bbd003413190fa40de8dcfd44df3e28a5d"},"seconds":64.34855055809021,"seed":913484493214453080,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\(\\sin^{-1}(\\sin 6x) = \\cos^{-1}(\\cos x)\\) for \\(x\\) in the interval \\([0, \\pi]\\), we need to understand the behavior of the inverse trigonometric functions \\(\\sin^{-1}\\) and \\(\\cos^{-1}\\).\n\nThe function \\(\\sin^{-1}(y)\\) gives the angle \\(\\theta\\) in the range \\([- \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]\\) such that \\(\\sin(\\theta) = y\\). Similarly, the function \\(\\cos^{-1}(y)\\) gives the angle \\(\\theta\\) in the range \\([0, \\pi]\\) such that \\(\\cos(\\theta) = y\\).\n\nFirst, let's analyze the left side of the equation, \\(\\sin^{-1}(\\sin 6x)\\). The value of \\(\\sin^{-1}(\\sin 6x)\\) will be the angle in \\([- \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]\\) that has the same sine value as \\(6x\\). So, we can write:\n\\[\n\\sin^{-1}(\\sin 6x) = 6x - 2k\\pi \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\pi - (6x - 2k\\pi) \\quad \\text{for some integer } k,\n\\]\nwhere \\(6x - 2k\\pi\\) is adjusted to fall within \\([- \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]\\).\n\nThe right side of the equation, \\(\\cos^{-1}(\\cos x)\\), simplifies to \\(x\\) if \\(x\\) is in \\([0, \\pi]\\), because \\(\\cos^{-1}(\\cos x) = x\\) for \\(x\\) in \\([0, \\pi]\\).\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\sin^{-1}(\\sin 6x) = x.\n\\]\nThis implies that \\(x\\) must be in the range \\([0, \\pi]\\) and \\(\\sin^{-1}(\\sin 6x)\\) must equal \\(x\\). Therefore, \\(6x\\) must be such that when reduced to the interval \\([- \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]\\), it equals \\(x\\).\n\nLet's consider the possible values of \\(6x\\) modulo \\(2\\pi\\). We need to find \\(x\\) such that:\n\\[\n6x = x + 2k\\pi \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 6x = \\pi - x + 2k\\pi,\n\\]\nfor some integer \\(k\\). Simplifying these, we get:\n\\[\n5x = 2k\\pi \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 7x = \\pi + 2k\\pi.\n\\]\nFrom \\(5x = 2k\\pi\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{2k\\pi}{5}.\n\\]\nFrom \\(7x = \\pi + 2k\\pi\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{(2k+1)\\pi}{7}.\n\\]\nWe need to check which of these values of \\(x\\) lie in the interval \\([0, \\pi]\\).\n\nFor \\(x = \\frac{2k\\pi}{5}\\):\n\\[\nk = 0, 1, 2 \\quad \\text{(since } \\frac{4\\pi}{5} < \\pi \\text{ but } \\frac{6\\pi}{5} > \\pi\\text{)}.\n\\]\nSo, the valid solutions are \\(x = 0, \\frac{2\\pi}{5}, \\frac{4\\pi}{5}\\).\n\nFor \\(x = \\frac{(2k+1)\\pi}{7}\\):\n\\[\nk = 0, 1, 2, 3 \\quad \\text{(since } \\frac{7\\pi}{7} = \\pi \\text{ but } \\frac{9\\pi}{7} > \\pi\\text{)}.\n\\]\nSo, the valid solutions are \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{7}, \\frac{3\\pi}{7}, \\frac{5\\pi}{7}, \\frac{7\\pi}{7} = \\pi\\).\n\nCombining all these solutions, we have:\n\\[\nx = 0, \\frac{\\pi}{7}, \\frac{2\\pi}{5}, \\frac{3\\pi}{7}, \\frac{4\\pi}{5}, \\frac{5\\pi}{7}, \\pi.\n\\]\nThus, there are 7 distinct solutions.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{7}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
